Dear Nadia Zahmoul
 
RE: RESPONSE TO NOTICE OF COMMENCEMENT OF PROCEEDINGS - Nadia Zahmoul v SSJ & HMCTS (AC-2025-LON-004293) -  Judicial Review Concerning Equality Act & Evidence Handling Failure
 
We write to acknowledge your email, dated 12th December 2025 in which you gave us notice of commencement of proceedings. This information allows us to understand the discrimination issues that people are facing and may help to shape our plans for future work.
 
The requirement to notify us of commencement of proceedings in England and Wales extends to equality claims in the county court only. In Scotland, the requirement to notify us extends to equality claims in the sheriff court or a disability discrimination claim in the First-Tier Tribunal (Additional Support Needs). However, if you have notified us of an equality claim issued in a different forum, we will keep a record of the information you have provided. If the claim is against your employer or former employer we may include your case in the statistics of our annual whistleblowing report.
 
It would be very helpful if you could let us know the outcome of the proceedings and send us a copy of any judgment or non-confidential terms of settlement from them.
 
If you would like advice about your case, you may wish to contact the Equality Advisory and Support Service (EASS). EASS is commissioned by the UK Government to provide advice services to individuals who have experienced discrimination or a breach of their human rights.
 
The EASS helpline is open Monday to Friday 9am to 5pm and Saturday 10am to 2pm. You can contact EASS in one of the following ways.
Freephone telephone: 0808 800 0082
Website: Equality Advisory and Support Service
Post: FREEPOST EASS HELPLINE FPN6521
Our privacy notice explains how we handle, store, use and share your personal information. If you have sent information to us on behalf of someone else, please signpost them to this notice.

Kind regards

Regulatory Hub Team
Equality and Human Rights Commission
 
Arndale House, The Arndale Centre
Manchester, M4 3AQ
 
equalityhumanrights.com
 


 


